{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "In which ONE of these substances do the constituent ions have the same electron configuration?\n\nA. KBr\nB. Na₂S\nC. MgCl₂\nD. CaCl₂"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is stuck, so begin with one small step: identify the ions in a familiar option and their charges. Do not yet compare all options or reveal the answer.",
      "content": "We can work this out by comparing how many electrons each ion has. Let’s start with option A, KBr: what charge do you think potassium and bromine ions have?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Maybe K is +1 and Br is -1? I don't know how that tells us if they have the same configuration."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly identifies the charges but needs the link between charge and electron count. Explain that same electron count means same electron configuration, then compare the ions in each option. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"20 - 2\") -> 18]",
      "content": "You’ve identified the ions in KBr correctly: K⁺ and Br⁻. The next step is to count electrons. A positive ion has lost electrons; a negative ion has gained them. If two ions have the same number of electrons, they have the same electron configuration.\n\nUse the atomic numbers and the ion charges:\n- **A. KBr:** K⁺ has 19 − 1 = 18 electrons; Br⁻ has 35 + 1 = 36.\n- **B. Na₂S:** Na⁺ has 11 − 1 = 10 electrons; S²⁻ has 16 + 2 = 18.\n- **C. MgCl₂:** Mg²⁺ has 12 − 2 = 10 electrons; Cl⁻ has 17 + 1 = 18.\n- **D. CaCl₂:** Ca²⁺ has 20 − 2 = 18 electrons; Cl⁻ has 17 + 1 = 18.\n\n**Final answer: D. CaCl₂** — its Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions each have 18 electrons, so they have the same electron configuration.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $N_e = Z - q$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $N_e$: the number of electrons in the ion\n  * $Z$: the atomic number, which is the number of protons in the neutral atom\n  * $q$: the ion charge, written with its sign (for example, $+2$ or $-1$)\n* **Why we use it:** A positive charge means electrons have been lost, and a negative charge means electrons have been gained. Subtracting the signed charge from the atomic number gives the ion’s electron count. Ions with equal electron counts have the same electron configuration.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Show the electron count for both ions in the chosen substance. Remember that anions gain electrons, so add the size of a negative charge to the atomic number."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "D",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nions = {\n    \"A\": (sp.Integer(19) - 1, sp.Integer(35) + 1),\n    \"B\": (sp.Integer(11) - 1, sp.Integer(16) + 2),\n    \"C\": (sp.Integer(12) - 2, sp.Integer(17) + 1),\n    \"D\": (sp.Integer(20) - 2, sp.Integer(17) + 1),\n}\nprint(next(option for option, counts in ions.items() if counts[0] == counts[1]))",
    "techniques": [
      "check_by_substitution",
      "given_formula_substitute_answer"
    ]
  }
}

{
